    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-66675",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d02418c3-4c04-57b8-8f10-18858b1759ab",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cotangent Function",
      "problem": "If $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ for some $x$, then find the value of $\\sqrt{3}cot⁡(x-\\pi )-4.$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡x=cot⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-1$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡x=cot⁡(x+(-1)\\cdot \\pi )=cot⁡(x-\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{3},$ then $cot⁡(x-\\pi )=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}cot⁡(x-\\pi )-4 & =\\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3})-4 \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =3-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
